  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 204 PM MDT Fri Aug 14 2026\n\nMonday, our region is forecast to be in a mostly zonal upper-level \nflow with a deepening trough developing off the west coast. High \ntemperatures are forecast in the 80s with lows in the 50s to 60s. \nThe southeast county warning area (CWA) could see some showers and \nthunderstorms Monday. Low confidence in severe weather occurring as \ninstability is overall weak and Probability of Precipitation (PoPs) \nmax out around 20%. Depending on the coverage of precipitation over \nthe weekend, localized flooding may be a concern with any storms \nthat form.\n\nTuesday through the remainder of the forecast period is expected to \nbe unseasonably warm. High temperatures are forecast in the 80s to \n90s for the CWA. We are forecast to remain in a mostly zonal flow \nuntil the trough off the west coast moves through our region and \ndisrupts our upper-level flow. There are chances for scattered \nevening showers and thunderstorms due to embedded shortwave \npassages. PoPs are around 20% each evening. Wednesday evening looks \nto be our best chance for precipitation with PoPs maxing out around \n40%. \n\nModels are uncertain on the track and evolution of the deepening \ntrough off the west coast, but it is likely to begin moving east \ntowards our region by Thursday. If the trough moves through sooner, \nit would likely take us out of a zonal pattern into a more northwest \nflow increasing our precipitation chances. It could also shift more \nnorth and put us back into a ridge pattern, which could shift us \ninto a ridge pattern and decrease our precipitation chances.",
